The Two Cultures & The Flexible Trade-Off



Data Modeling Culture (Classical Statistics)

Assumes data arise from a specified stochastic/mechanistic process (e.g., GLMMs, ODEs).
Primary goal: Parameter inference, uncertainty quantification, and causal interpretation.





↔︎




Algorithmic Modeling Culture (Machine Learning)

Treats the underlying data-generating mechanism as unknown or too complex to write analytically.
Primary goal: Out-of-sample predictive accuracy across high-dimensional, non-linear interactions.






&lt;div class="spec-title" style="color: #006f7a;"&gt;White Box · High Interpretability&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-eq"&gt;&lt;i&gt;Y&lt;/i&gt; = &beta;&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; + &beta;&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;i&gt;X&lt;/i&gt; &nbsp;|&nbsp; GUTS ODEs (&lt;i&gt;k&lt;sub&gt;d&lt;/sub&gt;, z, b&lt;sub&gt;w&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/i&gt;)&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-reg"&gt;Transparent mechanism &rarr; directly defensible in regulation.&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div class="spec-title" style="color: #005a8c;"&gt;Grey Box · Interpretable ML &amp; XAI&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-eq"&gt;Decision Trees · Random Forests + Feature Importance&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-reg"&gt;Captures non-linear patterns while auditing key drivers.&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;div class="spec-title" style="color: #8c1d18;"&gt;Black Box · High Predictive Capacity&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-eq"&gt;&lt;i&gt;ŷ&lt;/i&gt; = &lt;i&gt;f&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;&theta;&lt;/sub&gt;(&lt;i&gt;X&lt;/i&gt;) (millions of weights &amp; interactions)&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="spec-reg"&gt;Higher quantitative accuracy, opaque &rarr; harder to justify in regulation.&lt;/div&gt;



A Pragmatic, Objective-Driven Choice: Interpretability is not a dogma—it is an explicit scientific decision. High-throughput screening (e.g., QSARs) may justify prioritizing accuracy; regulatory or mechanistic risk assessment demands explainability. Increasingly, hybrid pipelines combine both.


Breiman, L. (2001). Statistical modeling: The two cultures. Statistical Science, 16(3), 199–231. 10.1214/ss/1009213726 · Rudin, C. (2019). Stop explaining black box machine learning models for high stakes decisions and use interpretable models instead. Nature Machine Intelligence, 1, 206–215. 10.1038/s42256-019-0048-x



Spoken core: Walk left-to-right across the spectrum: White Box models (linear regression, mechanistic GUTS ODEs) expose biological parameters and are directly defensible to regulators; Black Box models maximize predictive accuracy across millions of weights but are opaque. Click to emphasize flexibility—do not treat interpretability as dogma; match the tool to the objective. Note briefly why my PhD deliberately stays in the left/center zone (GUTS + interpretable ML) rather than jumping to deep learning.
